{
  "intro": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "Software can now pull readable text off websites and hand it to artificial intelligence systems, which use that text to learn and answer questions. Anyone weighing that task should know about the wider business behind it. Collecting information from websites by automated means has become a routine business activity rather than an occasional one-off task. Companies gather public web pages to study competitors, track prices, follow customer reviews and understand what shoppers want. Much of this work now runs through rented online services, so companies do not have to own and maintain the equipment themselves. The [[CMI_LINK:Web Scraping Services Market]] covers the firms that supply these services and the businesses that buy them.",
      "[[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]]"
    ]
  },
  "transition": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "Pulling readable text from websites is one slice of a wider paid market for web data. In that market, specialist firms sell the collection itself: they run the software, route requests through many machines and deliver clean results to clients. Buyers range from small research teams to large retailers, and they pay because gathering web data reliably takes time and equipment they would rather not run themselves.",
      "The sections below set out how large this market is, what is changing inside it and where it is heading."
    ]
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "sop": "report_context",
      "heading": "How big is the web scraping services market?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The market was worth USD 480 million in 2025, according to intelmarketresearch.com. Intelmarketresearch.com forecasts the market will reach USD 910 million by 2034. That forecast implies a compound annual growth rate of 7.3% from 2026 to 2034, intelmarketresearch.com reports. The size of the web scraping services market matters because it shows a steady, unspectacular climb rather than a sudden boom. Demand for web data extraction keeps the market moving upward as more companies treat collected pages as a normal input to their work."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "trends",
      "heading": "What is changing in the web scraping services market right now?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Three forces are reshaping the market at once: artificial intelligence is spreading through collection work, website defences are getting harder to beat, and site owners are pushing back. These trends in the web scraping services market point to a more managed, more cautious way of collecting data.\n- AI-assisted extraction is spreading but not yet standard. Apify and The Web Scraping Club's State of Web Scraping 2026 survey, published in January 2026, found that 54.2% of respondents do not use AI in their scraping workflows, while 45.8% do; among respondents, 66.2% said they plan to try AI-assisted tools.\n- Anti-bot defences are getting harder to beat. Grepsr reported in April 2026 that anti-bot systems have evolved from simple rule-based blocking into multi-layered defences powered by behavioural analysis, fingerprinting and machine learning, which makes scraping more complex and less predictable.\n- Website owners are blocking bots and pushing back legally. A growing number of sites put technical barriers in place during 2026 to keep AI agents off their content, emergent.sh reported in October 2026, and the Communications of the ACM reported the same month that objecting owners can now invoke anti-circumvention laws alongside breach of contract and copyright claims."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "segmentation",
      "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Segmentation",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The CMI report divides the web scraping services market segmentation into three parts: how the service is delivered, what it is used for, and where buyers are based.\n- By delivery type, the market splits into browser extensions, installable software and cloud based scraping.\n- By application, buyers use these services for data aggregation, market research, customer insight and other tasks.\n- By region, the market covers North America, Europe, Asia Pacific, Latin America, the Middle East and Africa."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "subsegment",
      "heading": "Sub-Segment Analysis of Web Scraping Services",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "segment": "type",
          "sub_segment": "Cloud Based",
          "paragraph": "Cloud-based delivery drives adoption because it removes the need to own and maintain equipment. A buyer rents collection capacity online and pays for what is used. AIJourn compared published pricing in October 2026 and found Firecrawl's Standard plan at $83 per month for 100,000 credits and ZenRows' Launch plan at $58 for 250,000 credits. AIJourn also noted that one page costs one credit, JSON extraction adds four credits, and pages returning an error still consume a credit. Rented capacity of this kind creates opportunities in the web scraping services market for providers that price clearly and keep results reliable."
        },
        {
          "segment": "application",
          "sub_segment": "Market Research",
          "paragraph": "Market research use turns collected pages into decisions. Teams gather competitor pages, prices and reviews, then study them to judge demand and position. The CMI report lists market research among the main applications of the web scraping services market, and the question of web scraping for market research how large the payoff can be depends on how cleanly the data arrives. Research buyers care less about raw volume than about results they can trust, which ties this sub-segment back to the wider market for dependable web data."
