### 2. trends  |  heading: "What is changing in the web scraping services market right now?"  |  about 249 words
Purpose (what the reader learns here): Three forces are reshaping the market: tougher anti-bot defenses, a shift to AI-assisted and managed collection, and growing legal and compliance pressure.
The heading is a question people really search. Answer it directly in the first one or two sentences (about 40 to 60 words), in plain words, then continue with the section.
Facts to use:
- [W6] Grepsr reported that anti-bot systems in 2026 have evolved from simple rule-based blocking into multi-layered defenses powered by behavioral analysis, fingerprinting and machine learning. (source: grepsr.com, 2026-04)
    detail: Grepsr says this shift has made scraping more complex, less predictable and more resource intensive, pushing enterprises to rethink how they design and operate data collection systems.
- [W10] Browserless reported that web scraping in 2026 is shifting toward AI-driven, cloud-based and compliance-aware methods, moving teams away from selector-heavy DIY approaches. (source: browserless.io, 2026-01)
    detail: The report attributes the shift to three forces: AI-native extraction, tougher anti-bot systems, and rising compliance pressure, which it says are pushing more teams toward real browsers and managed environments.
- [W9] Apify and The Web Scraping Club's State of Web Scraping 2026 survey found that 54.2% of respondents do not use AI in their scraping workflows, while 45.8% do. (source: blog.apify.com, 2026-01)
    detail: Among respondents, 66.2% said they plan to try AI-assisted web scraping tools, while 33.8% said they do not plan to use them even in the future.
- [W4] The Communications of the ACM reported that website owners objecting to scraping may now invoke anti-circumvention laws, codified in the U.S. as 17 U.S.C., alongside breach of contract, copyright and computer hacking theories. (source: Communications of the ACM, 2026-10)
    detail: The article notes that many earlier legal challenges to scraping publicly accessible sites failed, giving rise to a general understanding that such scraping is lawful, and that the obscure technical protection measure laws are new arrows for objecting site owners.
- [W1] A growing number of websites implemented technical barriers during 2026 to prevent AI agents from accessing their content. (source: emergent.sh, 2026-10)
    detail: Publishers cited unauthorized scraping that depletes server resources without attribution or compensation, and many report that AI crawlers generate substantial bandwidth costs without reciprocal traffic referrals.
Keywords:
  - REQUIRED secondary keyword: one sentence of this section must contain all three words "trends", "web scraping services" and "market" (reorder them as the sentence needs). A synonym does not count: an "opening" is not "opportunities", "how big" is not "size", and a sentence that says "the main drivers" without naming the market does not carry the keyword. Put that sentence in one paragraph only.
  - Related terms (optional): cloud based scraping.
Guest example (optional, at most one sentence, only if it makes the point clearer): "Websites that build their pages on the fly are harder to read automatically, so organisations often need more than one method of collection."
SHAPE: One paragraph string: a single lead-in sentence, then exactly three lines (never two, never four) that each start with "\n- ", one distinct trend per line (what is changing and what it does to the market, resting on a listed fact where one is given). The lead-in and the three lines are the whole paragraph.

