nt": "Cloud Based", "paragraph": "Cloud-based delivery carries much of the change in the Web Scraping Services Market, because rented capacity spares buyers the cost of running their own machines. Pricing shows how the model works. AIJourn reported in October 2026 that Firecrawl's Standard plan costs $83 a month for 100,000 credits, while ZenRows' Launch plan costs $58 for 250,000 credits. AIJourn also noted that a page returning an error still uses a credit. Use-Apify reported in March 2026 that extraction run by a language model adds $0.001 to $0.01 per page, with slower throughput than fixed rules. The opportunities in the web scraping services market therefore sit with providers who can keep those per-page costs low."}, {"segment": "application", "sub_segment": "Market Research", "paragraph": "Market research use stands out because the work is repetitive and the output feeds decisions directly. Teams compare prices, watch reviews and track rivals, and they need the same fields filled the same way each time. Use-Apify reported in March 2026 that fixed rules still win for high-volume, price-sensitive pipelines, while language-model extraction suits variable layouts and quick trials. Research buyers often need both, which keeps demand spread across delivery types."}]}, {"sop": "country_us", "heading": "Which region leads the web scraping services market?", "paragraphs": ["North America led this market, holding a major share in 2018 on the strength of a large number of key players based there. Asia Pacific is expected to be the fastest growing region for web scraping services as more companies there take up automated collection. The regional picture matters to buyers because support, pricing and legal advice tend to follow where vendors cluster. North America's early lead gave it the deepest bench of providers, and that depth still shapes how the market is served. Asia Pacific's expected pace points to new demand rather than replaced demand, since fast-growing regions usually add buyers rather than take them from elsewhere. Site owners in the United States are also tightening access. Cloudflare announced in June 2026 that from September 15 its default settings would block mixed-use crawlers from pages that host ads. Meta removed a controversial scraping clause from its commercial terms in October 2026, TechJuice reported, after legal experts and the public pushed back."]}, {"sop": "key_players", "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Key Players", "paragraphs": ["The field is fragmented. The report names a long list of vendors, and no single company holds the market in its grip. Buyers therefore choose from many suppliers with different strengths. The competitive landscape of the web scraping services market includes companies that offer web scraping services across automation, extraction and software platforms. Named vendors include Phantombuster, Mozenda, Diggernaut, Parseur, UiPath and PilotFish, among others such as Datahut, SysNucleus and Octopus Data."]}, {"sop": "conclusion", "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Outlook", "paragraphs": ["The outlook for the web scraping services market is steady growth from a modest base, with the total roughly doubling over the decade to 2034. Collection is moving away from hand-built setups and toward managed, AI-ready services, because tougher anti-bot defenses and tighter legal rules make DIY work harder to sustain. Cloud-based delivery and market research use show where that demand is most visible, and North America's early lead sits alongside Asia Pacific's expected faster pace. Buyers face a crowded field of vendors and rising compliance risk, so reliability and clear terms will count as much as price. A CMI analyst expects the vendors that thrive to be the ones that treat clean, lawful data as the product rather than an afterthought."]}]}
INFO:revamp:[write] PARSED JSON:
{
  "intro": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "Software that reads text off websites has become a working tool for artificial intelligence. A company points it at a page, and the words come back in a form a model can use. The task sounds small until you try it at scale, because websites change, block visitors and load content in ways a simple program cannot follow. Many teams now rent the collection instead of building it, and that rented work sits inside the [[CMI_LINK:Web Scraping Services Market]]. Collecting information from websites by automated means has become a routine business activity rather than an occasional one-off task. Companies gather public web pages to study competitors, track prices, follow customer reviews and understand what shoppers want. Much of this work now runs through rented online services, so companies do not have to own and maintain the equipment themselves.",
      "[[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]]"
    ]
  },
  "transition": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "Rented collection is only one part of a larger business. The market covers the tools and services that fetch public web pages, turn them into usable records and keep the flow running. Buyers are companies that need competitor prices, review text or product details without hiring a team to build the plumbing. Demand grows as more of that work feeds artificial intelligence systems, which need clean, steady supplies of text. The sections below set out how large this market is, what is changing inside it and where it is strongest."
    ]
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "sop": "report_context",
      "heading": "How big is the web scraping services market?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The market reached USD 480 million in 2025, intelmarketresearch.com reports. That is a modest figure for a business activity so widely used, and it reflects how many companies still build collection in-house rather than buy it. The size of the web scraping services market is set to grow steadily, according to intelmarketresearch.com, which puts the total at USD 910 million by 2034. intelmarketresearch.com expects growth of 7.3% a year between 2026 and 2034. A steady pace of that kind roughly doubles the market over the decade, which fits a service that more teams adopt as their data needs deepen."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "trends",
      "heading": "What is changing in the web scraping services market right now?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Three forces are reshaping how collection gets done: harder defenses on websites, a move toward AI-assisted and managed work, and tighter legal pressure. The trends in the web scraping services market all point the same way, toward fewer do-it-yourself setups.\n- Anti-bot systems have grown tougher. Grepsr reported in April 2026 that blocking has moved from simple rules to layered defenses that study behavior, identify machines and learn, which makes collection less predictable and more costly to run.\n- Collection is moving to AI-assisted and managed methods. Browserless reported in January 2026 that teams are leaving selector-heavy DIY work behind for real browsers and managed environments, pushed by AI-native extraction, tougher defenses and compliance pressure.\n- Legal and compliance risk is rising. The Communications of the ACM reported in October 2026 that site owners can now invoke anti-circumvention laws, alongside contract, copyright and hacking claims, against scrapers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "segmentation",
      "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Segmentation",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The report splits the web scraping services market segmentation into three parts, each cutting the market a different way.\n- By delivery type, buyers choose between a browser extension, installable software and cloud based scraping.\n- By application, the uses run from data aggregation and market research to customer insight and other tasks.\n- By region, the market covers North America, Europe, Asia Pacific, Latin America, the Middle East and Africa."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "subsegment",
      "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Sub-Segment Analysis",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "segment": "type",
          "sub_segment": "Cloud Based",
          "paragraph": "Cloud-based delivery carries much of the change in the Web Scraping Services Market, because rented capacity spares buyers the cost of running their own machines. Pricing shows how the model works. AIJourn reported in October 2026 that Firecrawl's Standard plan costs $83 a month for 100,000 credits, while ZenRows' Launch plan costs $58 for 250,000 credits. AIJourn also noted that a page returning an error still uses a credit. Use-Apify reported in March 2026 that extraction run by a language model adds $0.001 to $0.01 per page, with slower throughput than fixed rules. The opportunities in the web scraping services market therefore sit with providers who can keep those per-page costs low."
        },
        {
          "segment": "application",
          "sub_segment": "Market Research",
          "paragraph": "Market research use stands out because the work is repetitive and the output feeds decisions directly. Teams compare prices, watch reviews and track rivals, and they need the same fields filled the same way each time. Use-Apify reported in March 2026 that fixed rules still win for high-volume, price-sensitive pipelines, while language-model extraction suits variable layouts and quick trials. Research buyers often need both, which keeps demand spread across delivery types."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "country_us",
      "heading": "Which region leads the web scraping services market?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "North America led this market, holding a major share in 2018 on the strength of a large number of key players based there. Asia Pacific is expected to be the fastest growing region for web scraping services as more companies there take up automated collection. The regional picture matters to buyers because support, pricing and legal advice tend to follow where vendors cluster. North America's early lead gave it the deepest bench of providers, and that depth still shapes how the market is served. Asia Pacific's expected pace points to new demand rather than replaced demand, since fast-growing regions usually add buyers rather than take them from elsewhere. Site owners in the United States are also tightening access. Cloudflare announced in June 2026 that from September 15 its default settings would block mixed-use crawlers from pages that host ads. Meta removed a controversial scraping clause from its commercial terms in October 2026, TechJuice reported, after legal experts and the public pushed back."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "key_players",
      "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Key Players",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The field is fragmented. The report names a long list of vendors, and no single company holds the market in its grip. Buyers therefore choose from many suppliers with different strengths. The competitive landscape of the web scraping services market includes companies that offer web scraping services across automation, extraction and software platforms. Named vendors include Phantombuster, Mozenda, Diggernaut, Parseur, UiPath and PilotFish, among others such as Datahut, SysNucleus and Octopus Data."
      ]
    },
    {
      "sop": "conclusion",
      "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Outlook",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The outlook for the web scraping services market is steady growth from a modest base, with the total roughly doubling over the decade to 2034. Collection is moving away from hand-built setups and toward managed, AI-ready services, because tougher anti-bot defenses and tighter legal rules make DIY work harder to sustain. Cloud-based delivery and market research use show where that demand is most visible, and North America's early lead sits alongside Asia Pacific's expected faster pace. Buyers face a crowded field of vendors and rising compliance risk, so reliability and clear terms will count as much as price. A CMI analyst expects the vendors that thrive to be the ones that treat clean, lawful data as the product rather than an afterthought."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
