{"intro": {"paragraphs": ["Software can now pull readable text off websites and hand it to artificial intelligence systems, which use that text to learn and answer questions. Anyone weighing that task should know about the wider business behind it. Collecting information from websites by automated means has become a routine business activity rather than an occasional one-off task. Companies gather public web pages to study competitors, track prices, follow customer reviews and understand what shoppers want. Much of this work now runs through rented online services, so companies do not have to own and maintain the equipment themselves. The [[CMI_LINK:Web Scraping Services Market]] covers the firms that supply this kind of collection.", "[[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]]"]}, "transition": {"paragraphs": ["That everyday need to collect web data at scale is what the web scraping services market sells. Buyers are companies that want competitor prices, review sentiment or product details without hiring a team to build collection systems from scratch. Vendors sell the tools, the rented infrastructure and the managed service that keep those collections running. The sections below set out the market's size, its direction and the companies that compete in it."]}, "sections": [{"sop": "report_context", "heading": "How big is the web scraping services market?", "paragraphs": ["The web scraping services market is worth USD 480 million in 2025, according to intelmarketresearch.com. That figure covers what buyers spend on tools and services that collect public web data. The size of the web scraping services market points to steady demand rather than a sudden boom. Intelmarketresearch.com forecasts the market will reach USD 910 million by 2034. Intelmarketresearch.com puts growth at 7.3% a year between 2026 and 2034. A steady pace like that suggests buyers keep adding collection work as their data needs widen."]}, {"sop": "trends", "heading": "What is changing in the web scraping services market right now?", "paragraphs": ["Three shifts stand out: AI-assisted extraction is spreading but not yet standard, anti-bot defenses are getting harder to beat, and website owners are pushing back.\n- AI-assisted extraction is spreading without becoming the norm. Apify and The Web Scraping Club's State of Web Scraping 2026 survey found that 54.2% of respondents do not use AI in their scraping workflows, while 45.8% do. Among those respondents, 66.2% said they plan to try AI-assisted web scraping tools.\n- Anti-bot systems have grown tougher. Grepsr reported in April 2026 that anti-bot systems have evolved from simple rule-based blocking into multi-layered defenses powered by behavioral analysis, fingerprinting and machine learning. Grepsr says this shift has made scraping more complex and less predictable, which pushes enterprises to rethink how they design data collection. These trends in the web scraping services market favor vendors that run real browsers and managed environments.\n- Website owners are blocking bots and pushing back legally. The Communications of the ACM reported in October 2026 that site owners may now invoke anti-circumvention laws alongside breach of contract and copyright claims. Meta removed a controversial web scraping clause from its commercial terms of use on October 17, 2026, after legal experts and the public pushed back."]}, {"sop": "segmentation", "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Segmentation", "paragraphs": ["The CMI report splits the web scraping services market segmentation into three parts: how the tool is delivered, what the data is used for, and where the buyer sits.\n- By delivery type, buyers choose a browser extension, installable software or a cloud based tool.\n- By application, the data serves data aggregation, market research, customer insight and other uses.\n- By region, the market covers North America, Europe, Asia Pacific, Latin America, the Middle East and Africa."]}, {"sop": "subsegment", "heading": "Web Scraping Services Sub-Segment Analysis", "paragraphs": [{"segment": "type", "sub_segment": "Cloud Based", "paragraph": "Cloud based delivery draws teams that would rather rent extraction than run their own tools. Published pricing shows how that rental works. AIJourn compared published pricing across web scraping APIs for AI agents in October 2026, including Firecrawl's Standard plan at $83 per month for 100,000 credits and ZenRows' Launch plan at $58 for 250,000 credits. AIJourn notes that one page costs one credit, JSON extraction adds four credits, and pages returning 403 or 404 still consume one credit. Use-Apify reported in March 2026 that LLM-based extraction adds per-page inference costs of $0.001 to $0.01 depending on model and page length. Those per-page costs create openings in the web scraping services market for vendors that keep managed plans predictable."}, {"segment": "application", "sub_segment": "Market Research", "paragraph": "Market research is a core use of the data teams collect. Companies pull competitor pages, prices and reviews to see how a category is moving, and they want that data in a form their models can read. Use-Apify reported in March 2026 that selectors still win for high-volume, price-sensitive pipelines, while LLM extraction is increasingly used for variable layouts and rapid prototyping. Market research data therefore shapes what vendors build, because buyers want clean output rather than raw pages."}]}, {"sop": "country_us", "heading": "Which region leads the web scraping services market?", "paragraphs": ["North America led the web scraping services market in 2018, when it held a major share owing to the presence of a large number of key players, according to the CMI report. Asia Pacific is expected to be the fastest growing region for web scraping services. Buyers in Asia Pacific are likely to see more vendor choice as that growth pulls suppliers into the region.\n- Cloudflare announced that starting September 15, 2026, its default settings will block mixed-use crawlers from any pages that host ads. Cloudflare said the change separates crawlers used for traditional search from those used for AI agents and training.\n- Webscraper.live reported in March 2026 that major websites, particularly news outlets, are aggressively blocking bots, especially those accessing content for AI training purposes. Webscraper.live says these bots often disregard terms of service and strain server resources."]}, {"sop": "key_players", "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Key Players", "paragraphs": ["The field looks fragmented. The CMI report names a long list of players, and no single one appears to hold a dominant share of the web scraping services market. That spread gives buyers plenty of vendors to compare on price and reliability.\nCompanies that offer web scraping services include Phantombuster, Mozenda, Diggernaut, Datahut, Parseur and UiPath, among others the report names."]}, {"sop": "conclusion", "heading": "Web Scraping Services Market Outlook", "paragraphs": ["The outlook for the web scraping services market is steady rather than dramatic. The forecast above points to a market roughly twice its current size by the middle of the next decade, and the pace stays modest. Demand from digital marketing data and from teams preparing information for AI systems keeps that growth moving. Buyers are also shifting toward managed, compliance-aware collection as anti-bot defenses and legal pressure raise the cost of doing it themselves. A CMI analyst expects vendors that handle blocking, scheduling and compliance for their customers to take the larger part of that growth."]}]}
