ects sophisticated digital experiences, and that demand keeps the U.S. industry booming, according to the CMI report. Many traditional U.S. firms white-label existing platforms or partner with FinTechs to offer automated, goal-based investing and integrated ESG capabilities, the CMI report says. Apex Fintech Solutions formed an alliance with FusionIQ in September 2026 to combine Apex's clearing, custody and brokerage infrastructure with FusionIQ's cloud-based digital wealth platform, Yahoo Finance reported. The pair aim to cut integration complexity and shorten time-to-market for banks, credit unions, registered investment advisors and broker-dealers modernising their services."]}, {"sop": "key_players", "heading": "Who are the leading companies in the wealth management platform market?", "paragraphs": ["The field is crowded and consolidating. Large vendors buy smaller specialists to add trading, portfolio management or custody, and scale plus AI capability increasingly decide who holds position. The competitive landscape of the wealth management platform market rewards firms that can serve many clients on one system.\nThe CMI report names Avaloq, Backbase, Broadridge Financial Solutions, FIS, Fiserv, InvestCloud, SEI Investments, SS&C Technologies, Tata Consultancy Services and Temenos among the key players, alongside Comarch, Dorsum, InvestEdge, Profile Software and Prometeia.\n- InvestCloud: FinTech Magazine named it among the top wealthtech platforms in October 2026, noting it supports more than US$9tn in assets.\n- SEI: Huntington Bank selected SEI's platform in March 2026 for a unified wealth management system, FinTech Magazine reported.\n- SS&C: more than 22,000 organisations rely on its software and services, FinTech Magazine reported.\n- Vanguard: it agreed to acquire Altruist, a digital platform and custodian for independent advisors, in a deal valuing Altruist at about $4 billion, fintechfutures reported in August 2026.\n- Envestnet: it agreed to acquire 