Purpose (what the reader learns here): North America leads with 36.4% of the market in 2026 while Asia Pacific grows fastest, and in the U.S. high-net-worth clients and younger investors are pushing firms toward digital, hybrid and ESG-ready platforms.
The heading is a question people really search. Answer it directly in the first one or two sentences (about 40 to 60 words), in plain words, then continue with the section.
Facts to use:
- [fs.regions.leader] Leading region: North America (36.4% of the market share in 2026) (CMI report)
- [fs.regions.fastest] Fastest-growing region: Asia Pacific (fastest growing market) (CMI report)
- [fs.us.0] The U.S. wealth management platform industry is booming because a growing population of high-net-worth individuals expects sophisticated digital experiences. (CMI report)
- [fs.us.1] Many traditional U.S. firms are white-labeling existing wealth management platforms or partnering with FinTechs to offer automated, goal-based investing and integrated ESG capabilities. (CMI report)
- [W9] Edward Jones launched a hybrid digital investment advice offering to make its wealth management platform more appealing to younger investors. (source: MSN, 2026-08)
- [W7] Broadridge Financial Solutions expanded its digital assets platform to U.S. wealth management firms, giving broker-dealers, RIAs and wealth managers a single environment for cryptocurrencies and tokenized securities alongside conventional investments. (source: InvestmentNews, 2026-09)
    detail: Broadridge said approximately 32% of financial advisors invested in crypto for client accounts in 2025, up from 22% previously.
Keywords:
  - REQUIRED long-tail search: one sentence of this section must contain every content word of "wealth management platform market size in north america" (reorder the words freely; only a, the, of, in, for, and may sit between them; never swap a word for a synonym). (rests on fs.regions.leader).
  - Related terms (optional): high net worth individuals.
SHAPE: Plain prose in one or two short paragraphs, or a lead-in sentence followed by "\n- " lines when each line rests on its own listed fact. It covers where the market is strongest and, where facts allow, the United States, in 100 to 200 words in all; fewer than 100 words is a miss, so carry the region picture with the listed facts plus your own plain sentences about what that picture means for buyers.

### 6. key_players  |  heading: "Who are the leading companies in the wealth management platform market?"  |  about 164 words
Purpose (what the reader learns here): The competitive landscape is crowded, with established technology vendors named in the report competing alongside firms that are consolidating and funding new AI-native platforms.
The heading is a question people really search. Answer it directly in the first one or two sentences (about 40 to 60 words), in plain words, then continue with the section.
Facts to use:
- [fs.players] Key players the report names: Avaloq, Backbase, Broadridge Financial Solutions, Comarch, Dorsum, FIS, Fiserv, InvestCloud, InvestEdge, Profile Software, Prometeia, SEI Investments, SS & C Technologies, Tata Consultancy Services, Temenos (CMI report)
- [W32] FinTech Magazine named InvestCloud, SEI, SS&C and Finastra among the top wealthtech platforms, noting InvestCloud supports more than US$9tn in assets with clients managing more than 40% of the global wealth market. (source: FinTech Magazine, 2026-10)
    detail: SEI's clients include Huntington Bank, which selected the platform in March 2026 for a unified wealth management system, and more than 22,000 organisations rely on SS&C's software and services.
- [W37] Envestnet agreed to acquire Vestmark, a provider of portfolio management technology, institutional-grade trading and outsourced investment management services. (source: Yahoo Finance, 2026-09)
    detail: Envestnet said it serves more than a third of all advisors across its platforms and $8 trillion in platform assets, while Vestmark supports more than $2 trillion in assets and five million-plus accounts.
- [W15] Digital wealth management firm Farther raised $150 million in a Series D funding round led by General Atlantic to scale its intelligent wealth platform. (source: fintechfutures, 2026-05)
- [W40] Orion earned ISO 42001, the international AI management system standard, validating the governance framework behind its Denali AI. (source: Business Wire, 2026-06)
    detail: Orion said the certification covers permissioned data, auditable outputs and human oversight built into the platforms advisors already use.
Keywords:
  - REQUIRED secondary keyword: one sentence of this section must contain all three words "competitive landscape", "wealth management platform" and "market" (reorder them as the sentence needs). A synonym does not count: an "opening" is not "opportunities", "how big" is not "size", and a sentence that says "the main drivers" without naming the market does not carry the keyword. Put that sentence in one paragraph only.
  - Related terms (optional): portfolio management software, investment management.
SHAPE: Two entries in "paragraphs". First: a short plain passage on how crowded or concentrated the field is. Second: the companies. A company with a listed fact gets one line "- <Company>: <what the fact says>" under a short lead-in clause; companies with no fact are named together in one plain sentence and are never given a detail the facts do not supply.

### 7. conclusion  |  heading: "Wealth Management Platform Market Outlook"  |  about 109 words
Purpose (what the reader learns here): Pulling the sections together: a market growing at double digits, pushed by robo and hybrid advice and AI, led by North America with Asia Pacific catching up, and contested by many vendors, with the clearest openings in robo advisory, AI analytics and expanding internet access in developing nations.
Facts to use:
- [fs.opportunities.2] Increasing internet proliferation in developing nations gives an impetus to the revenue growth of the wealth management platform market. (CMI report)
Keywords:
  - REQUIRED secondary keyword: one sentence of this section must contain all three words "outlook", "wealth management platform" and "market" (reorder them as the sentence needs). A synonym does not count: an "opening" is not "opportunities", "how big" is not "size", and a sentence that says "the main drivers" without naming the market does not carry the keyword. Put that sentence in one paragraph only.
SHAPE: One paragraph that pulls the post together using only points already made. It contains no digits at all: it refers back to earlier numbers in words (the forecast above, steady growth, a modest pace). It still uses the market's full name once. Its last sentence is the CMI analyst's view.

RULES THAT APPLY EVERYWHERE
- Voice: plain everyday English for a general reader. Short sentences with one idea each, averaging about 15 words and none longer than 25. Prefer the everyday word (use, show, buy, grow, help) to the formal one. A bullet is one or two short sentences, never one long one. Contractions are fine. No slang, hype, calls to action or direct address ("you"). The first time a technical term is unavoidable, explain it in a few words; if a sentence works without it, leave it out.
- Actors: say who does what. The subject of a sentence is the person, company, group or thing that acts (buyers, vendors, teams, regulators, a technology), and its verb is active. A source's name goes in a short clause beside the fact, never in a frame that makes a report or a website the subject of a sentence about the market. Use a passive verb only when nobody can be named as the actor. A sentence states a fact with its actor as the subject and an active verb; target at most one passive sentence in six.
- Clear reference: every sentence can be understood by itself. No sentence begins with a pointing word (this, that, these, those, such), whether or not a noun follows it, and none begins with a bare pronoun that refers back to an earlier sentence or idea. To connect a sentence to the one before it, name the thing again with its own noun as the subject, or join the two ideas with a connecting word inside one sentence. A reader who lands on any single sentence must know what it is about without reading the one before it.
- Facts: state only the facts listed for a section. A fact from the CMI report may be stated plainly. A fact whose source is not CMI (news evidence, or a figure marked NOT CMI) names that source, and the month when it has one, in the same sentence and is never credited to CMI. Every sentence that carries a number from such a fact names the source inside that very sentence, even when the sentence before it already did. Every sentence that gives one of another source's figures carries the source's name again in itself — "The same source", "That source" and "It" pointing back at a source are never written. A fact marked historical carries its year and is never presented as current. A figure appears once in the whole post: never repeat one from an earlier section, the conclusion included; refer back to it in words. A sentence that gives a number says what the number measures. Never write a figure, company detail or claim that no listed fact supports; where a section has few facts, it stays short. The "Purpose" line of a section tells you what the reader should learn; it is not a source of facts.
- Explaining: you may add plain sentences that explain what a fact means, what a term means or why it matters to the market, as long as they add no figure, name, date or claim of their own. This is how a section with few facts still reads as a full, useful section.
- Never talk about the writing process: no mention of "the facts", "the plan", "the report not saying", "what is not available" or what you could not find. Leave out what you cannot support, quietly.
- Placement: put each fact in the sentence that makes the section's point. At most one fact per sentence, and no chains of "according to".
- No filler: every clause carries information. A clause whose only job is to tell the reader what they will see, understand or learn, or to announce why a fact is being given, is left out. A sentence ends when its fact ends. A list lead-in states the split plainly (who splits what, into how many parts) and nothing about its purpose.
- Flow: each section's first sentence picks up from the section before it. After the transition the post stays on the market. The guest article's context belongs to the opening alone: a market section uses a guest point only when that section lists one as a guest example, and then in one sentence.
- Fresh writing: put every fact in your own words and sentence shapes; never reuse a run of ten or more words from a fact or the report as written — this includes lists of segment categories, which you break up by writing the connective words your own way and keeping a few of your own words between the split and its categories. A fact that arrives as a full sentenced statement (a driver, a restraint, a U.S. statement, a company note) is research wording, not a line of prose: say its meaning again in your own words, keeping only its figures; its sentence shape must not survive into the draft. Similarly, when a fact lists many companies, name only the few that matter in that sentence (at most six), grouped or ordered your own way, never the whole list in the order given.
- Primary keyword: use the full market name once in every market section, the conclusion included, and never twice in the same paragraph. The sentence that carries a section's secondary keyword may be that one use. Elsewhere in the section say "the market", "this market" or "it". It is plain text everywhere except the link in the opening. The market is an industry category made of many companies' products: never say or imply that it is a product, software or a company.
- Secondary keyword: where a section lists one, every word of it must appear together in one sentence of that section — that section is not finished without it, whatever its facts allow. Each keyword belongs to its section alone: its words sit in one paragraph there, never spread over two, and when the same idea comes up in other sections it is said in different words ("openings", "the biggest opening", the word the world uses), never in a listed keyword's words. You may reorder a keyword's words to fit the sentence; you may not replace one with a synonym. Use no keyword outside the section it is listed for, so a paragraph never ends up playing host to two sections' keywords.
- Long-tail search: where a section lists one, that section is not finished until one of its sentences contains every content word of it, whatever its facts allow. The words may be REORDERED to read well, with only light words (a, the, of, in, for, and) between them, and acronyms and proper nouns are written in their normal capitalisation (AI, US) even though the phrase is listed in lowercase; a synonym never counts. The sentence is about something real in the section: the phrase is never the subject of a sentence about itself, never a label, never a row of the sentence's own topic words, and never placed after a colon or comma.
- Related terms are optional: use one where it fits a sentence you would write anyway.
- CMI analyst opinion: one or two sentences in the whole post, no more. The conclusion's last sentence is always one. Attribute it by role ("a CMI analyst", "CMI's research team"), never by name or in quotation marks. It is a judgement a reader could disagree with, built only from facts already in the post, and it adds no new fact.
- Recency: call something recent, new or current only if its source places it within the 12 months before today's date.
- Length: the whole post comes to 1350 words, give or take 10 percent. Count roughly as you write: if the post is running to the outer ends of the section budgets, add a plain explanatory sentence where one already belongs, never filler; never hand in sections of half the budget.

OUTPUT CHECKLIST
All seven market sections are present in the given order with their given headings. The opening has the link once and the placeholder once. Every figure is in a listed fact. No section states a fact that is not listed. Every assigned keyword is in its section, and every section's listed long-tail phrase is used in that section.

