INFO:revamp:[write] USER PROMPT:
TITLE (final, already decided; you do not write or change it): Digital Marketing Software Market Shifts Toward AI-Led Tools
THE MARKET (the primary keyword, always written exactly like this): Digital Marketing Software Market
TODAY'S DATE: 2026-10-23

HOW THE POST IS BUILT
Three blocks in this order, and the post never goes back: an OPENING that uses a guest article's subject as an example, a short TRANSITION into the market, then the MARKET SECTIONS. Every block and section has a word budget: write up to it — each block and section lands between nine tenths and eleven tenths of its budget, because a short block is a half-written block, not a plain one.

THE OPENING (at most 239 words of your own, plus the retained text; this is a hard limit, so stay under it)
- Who it is for: a reader who has never seen the guest article, does not care about its details and knows nothing about the field. Everything that reader needs in order to follow the opening has to be in the opening itself.
- The guest article's subject: Marketing automation is the use of software to handle repetitive promotional work, and anyone curious about it would want to know about the market behind these tools because that is where the products and capabilities they would actually use come from.
- Context you may draw on, in your own words. Each statement was chosen because it helps a stranger understand the market the post goes on to cover; use them to explain what the activity is, who does it, why it matters and what is changing:
  - Marketing automation means using software to carry out routine promotional chores, such as sending emails and posting on social media, without a person doing each one by hand.
  - As a company grows, keeping up with these chores manually gets harder, so businesses turn to software to handle the volume.
  - These tools let a business sort customers into groups, send each group messages suited to them, and track how people respond.
- The retained paragraph is written by someone else and is placed by code. Never write its words or a paraphrase of them. Read it, so that your lead-in prepares the reader for whatever it takes for granted (a term, a comparison, something it refers back to). Its words are: "Customer data management systems"
- The retained text is a BARE LINKED PHRASE, not a paragraph: "Customer data management systems". Write intro paragraph 2 as a paragraph of your own in which the placeholder [[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]] appears exactly ONCE, INSIDE one of your sentences, as a noun phrase that sentence grammatically needs, with your own words before it and your own words after it in that same paragraph (illustrative shape only, not real content: "...and that review starts with [[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]] a team already runs, because ..."). The phrase is inserted exactly as written, including its capitalisation, so choose a sentence shape in which it reads naturally. Never repeat or paraphrase the phrase yourself anywhere else in the article.
- How to write it: begin with the plain idea (what the subject is and why anyone cares), build one step at a time, and when a sentence builds on the one before it, repeat the key noun as its own subject or join the two ideas with a connecting word inside a single sentence, instead of pointing back at the earlier sentence. Explain every word a stranger might not know the first time it appears, in everyday words. Leave out measurements, tool names, technical steps and anything only a specialist would care about.
- Build the opening as: a lead-in paragraph that sets the scene with the subject and names the market through the report link, inside its first 100 words; then a second paragraph of your own in which the placeholder sits INSIDE one of your sentences, with your own words before and after it (the retained text is a short linked phrase, not a paragraph, so it never stands alone on a line). Nothing else is needed. The link is written exactly once, as [[CMI_LINK:Digital Marketing Software Market]], and it is the first time the market's name appears.
- The opening states no market fact (no size, growth, share, segment or vendor).

THE TRANSITION (about 101 words, one or two short paragraphs, no heading; never fewer than 75 words — a shorter transition vanishes between the opening and the sections and falls below the share the plan gives this block)
- What it must do: The tools behind these routine marketing chores form a large and fast-changing software market, and its size, trends, segments and leaders are what this article explains next.
- Purpose: the reader should feel the turn from the example to the market as natural. Say in plain words what the market is (what is bought, who buys it and why), tie it to the need the opening described, then lead into the first market section. Do not describe the post itself and do not list what follows. It neither repeats the opening nor starts stating the market's facts.

THE MARKET SECTIONS (in this order; each heading is given and is final)
### 1. report_context  |  heading: "How big is the digital marketing software market?"  |  about 125 words
Purpose (what the reader learns here): The market is already worth USD 17.85 billion in 2026 and is forecast to reach USD 37.94 billion by 2033, growing at 11.2% a year.
The heading is a question people really search. Answer it directly in the first one or two sentences (about 40 to 60 words), in plain words, then continue with the section.
Facts to use:
- [fs.size] Market size: USD 17.85 billion (2026) (CMI report)
- [fs.forecast] Market forecast: USD 37.94 billion (2033) (CMI report)
- [fs.cagr] Growth rate (CAGR): 11.2% (2026 to 2033) (CMI report)
Keywords:
  - REQUIRED secondary keyword: one sentence of this section must contain all three words "forecast", "digital marketing software" and "market" (reorder them as the sentence needs). A synonym does not count: an "opening" is not "opportunities", "how big" is not "size", and a sentence that says "the main drivers" without naming the market does not carry the keyword. Put that sentence in one paragraph only.
  - REQUIRED long-tail search: one sentence of this section must contain every content word of "digital marketing software market growth rate" (reorder the words freely; only a, the, of, in, for, and may sit between them; never swap a word for a synonym). (rests on fs.cagr).
  - Related terms (optional): marketing automation.
SHAPE: One paragraph. It states the market's size and where it is heading only as far as the listed facts do.

### 2. trends  |  heading: "What are the latest trends in digital marketing software?"  |  about 216 words
Purpose (what the reader learns here): Three forces are reshaping the market: AI and machine learning raising personalization and conversion, omnichannel marketing pushing buyers toward unified suites, and real-time analytics cutting churn.
The heading is a question people really search. Answer it directly in the first one or two sentences (about 40 to 60 words), in plain words, then continue with the section.
Facts to use:
- [fs.drivers.0] The integration of AI and machine learning in marketing solutions enhances personalization and campaign effectiveness, with AI users achieving up to 30% higher conversion rates in 2026. (CMI report)
- [fs.drivers.2] The shift to omnichannel marketing requires sophisticated tools to unify cross-channel data, evidenced by a 25% increase in ROI among early adopters. (CMI report)
- [fs.drivers.3] Real-time analytics adoption reduces customer churn by 20%, boosting market growth. (CMI report)
- [W9] The average enterprise now runs over 90 marketing technology tools simultaneously, yet most CMOs admit they cannot clearly measure the ROI of more than half their stack. (source: mba-training.com, 2026-06)
- [W10] The marketing technology landscape has grown from 150 solutions in 2011 to 15,384 solutions, a 9% increase from 14,106, with 2,489 new tools qualifying in 2025 out of 11,133 candidates evaluated. (source: gtm8020.com, 2026-01)
    detail: The same compilation reports marketing budgets dropped to 7.7% of company revenue in 2024, down from 9.1% in 2023.
- [W3] An ITSMA survey found 71% of companies increased account-based marketing, with 50% of those surveyed saying they increased ABM staff. (source: martech.org, undated)
    detail: The coverage notes vendors are combining functions into suites: Demandbase calls its offering "Smarter GTM," Madison Logic calls its solution "Journey Acceleration," and Salesforce expects businesses to align marketing, sales and customer service on its Customer 360 Platform.
Keywords:
  - REQUIRED secondary keyword: one sentence of this section must contain all three words "trends", "digital marketing software" and "market" (reorder them as the sentence needs). A synonym does not count: an "opening" is not "opportunities", "how big" is not "size", and a sentence that says "the main drivers" without naming the market does not carry the keyword. Put that sentence in one paragraph only.
  - REQUIRED long-tail search: one sentence of this section must contain every content word of "omnichannel marketing software roi increase" (reorder the words freely; only a, the, of, in, for, and may sit between them; never swap a word for a synonym). (rests on fs.drivers.2).
  - Related terms (optional): ai in marketing, omnichannel marketing.
Guest example (optional, at most one sentence, only if it makes the point clearer): "Newer versions of these tools can draft sequences of messages and pick what to send based on what is known about each customer."
SHAPE: One paragraph string: a single lead-in sentence, then exactly three lines (never two, never four) that each start with "\n- ", one distinct trend per line (what is changing and what it does to the market, resting on a listed fact where one is given). The lead-in and the three lines are the whole paragraph.

### 3. segmentation  |  heading: "Digital Marketing Software Market Segmentation"  |  about 79 words
