{
  "intro": {
    "paragraphs": [
      "Marketing automation means letting software handle the repetitive parts of promotion, like sending emails or posting on social media, so no one has to do each task by hand. A small business can manage that work manually at first. As the business grows, the volume of messages, posts and follow-ups outgrows what a person can keep up with, and companies turn to software to carry the load. These tools also let a business sort customers into groups, send each group messages that suit it, and watch how people respond. Anyone curious about marketing automation is really asking about the products behind it, and those products come from the [[CMI_LINK:Digital Marketing Software Market]].",
      "The market is where buyers choose among tools built for different jobs, and the choice usually starts with the customer records a company already keeps. Most firms hold those records in [[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]] that feed names, purchase history and past replies into the marketing tools, so the software knows who should get which message. A company that keeps its records in order gets more from every tool it buys, and a company that does not often finds the software working harder than the people using it."
    ]
  },
  "transition": {
