    "paragraphs": [
      "Marketing automation is the practice of letting software handle the repetitive parts of promotion, like sending out emails or posting to social media, so no one has to do each task by hand. A small business can manage that work on its own at first. As a company grows, the volume of messages, posts and follow-ups outgrows any person's day, and businesses turn to software to keep up. These tools also let a business sort customers into groups, send each group messages that suit them, and watch how people respond. Anyone curious about marketing automation is really asking about the products behind it, and those products come from the [[CMI_LINK:Digital Marketing Software Market]].",
      "The tools rarely work alone. They lean on records about each buyer, and that work starts with [[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]] a company already keeps, because messages aimed at the wrong group waste money and patience alike. Software that reads those records well can decide who gets which message and when. Software that reads them badly just sends more noise. The difference shows up in how customers answer, and it explains why businesses treat these systems as core equipment rather than a side purchase."
